>
Ballroom, Near French Door
The detective is now near the fireplace.
The detective sees you, approaches warily, and grabs you firmly by the wrist, and with a practiced twist, slips the cuffs on you. "You're under arrest for the murder of Veronica Ashcroft." The standard warnings are given. Sergeant Duffy appears, as though out of nowhere, and escorts you out to the waiting police car. All your protests are ignored.

In the subsequent trial, you are acquitted, in spite of the fact that your lariat was the murder weapon and the bullet from your gunbelt was found near the body. Finally, several guests testified against you as a nosy and suspicious character.

However, the detective and Sergeant Duffy, nagged by doubt, continue the investigation on their own time. Their brilliant detective work unravels the tangled mess behind the murder. You are released, and as you leave jail, you can't help but think that if you had been able to figure out what was going on that night, you might have won yourself a Pulitzer Prize by now.

The story has come to an end.

